            <ArticleTitle>Delirium Associated With Lidocaine Patch Administration: A Case Presentation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>597-601</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2015.12.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(16)00002-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Delirium is a common complication among hospitalized elderly individuals. Systemic analgesics are known precipitating factors for delirium. However, the risk from topical pain agents is not well documented. We report a case of a woman who developed delirium after the application of a lidocaine patch. Although lidocaine patches are generally perceived as safe, clinicians should be aware of delirium as a potential adverse effect, particularly in older patients. This report also highlights the role of a systematic approach of staff education, use of a standardized protocol, and interdisciplinary communication in the identification and management of delirium in acute rehabilitation facilities.</AbstractText>
